-**Pagination.** Lists take `page` (default 1) and `per_page` (default 25, at
+**Pagination.** Every list comes in pages, by page number or by cursor.
+Cursors stay fast however deep you browse, and pages don't shift while others
+work, so use them for long lists such as tickets, comments and users.
+
+By page number, lists take `page` (default 1) and `per_page` (default 25, at
 most 100) and return `count` (total records), `page`, `per_page`, and
 `next_page`/`previous_page` (page numbers, or `null`). A value that isn't a
 positive integer is `400 bad_request`, and so is an unknown filter value
 such as `?status=bogus` (a blank filter is ignored). A page past the end is empty, and its
-`previous_page` is the last page.
+`previous_page` is the last page. Deeper pages take longer, because the
+database counts the list and skips every record before the page; and when
+records are added or move (a ticket with new activity jumps to the top), the
+following pages shift, repeating or skipping records.
+
+By cursor, ask for `page[size]` (default 25, at most 100), e.g.
+`GET /tickets?page[size]=25&status=open`. The records are as usual, with
+`meta` and `links` instead of the page numbers:
+
+```json
+{"tickets":[...],"meta":{"has_more":true,"after_cursor":"WyJ0aWNr...","before_cursor":"WyJ0aWNr..."},"links":{"next":"https://helpdesk.example.com/api/v1/tickets?page%5Bafter%5D=WyJ0aWNr...&page%5Bsize%5D=25&status=open","prev":null}}
+```
+
+* `before_cursor` and `after_cursor` are the cursors of the page's first and
+  last records (`null` for an empty page). `page[after]=<cursor>` with
+  `page[size]` gives the records after that record, and
+  `page[before]=<cursor>` the ones before it (the page that ends just before
+  it), in the list's usual order.
+* `has_more` says whether there are more records in the direction you're
+  browsing: after the page, or before it with `page[before]`.
+* `links.next` and `links.prev` are the URLs of the following and preceding
+  pages, with the same filters and size, or `null` if there's nothing that
+  way (the first page has no `prev`). An empty page, e.g. after the last
+  record, links back to the page ending with the cursor's record.
+* There's no `count`: counting a long list is what gets slow.
+* Cursors are opaque strings that don't expire. They hold the cursor record's
+  place in the order (e.g. its activity time and ID) as it was when listed,
+  so records being added, changed or deleted (even the cursor's own) never
+  make a page repeat or skip records. A record that moves, like a ticket with
+  new activity, shows up where it now belongs, e.g. back at the top of
+  `GET /tickets`. To check for new comments, ask for the comments after the
+  last cursor you have.
+
+`400 bad_request` for a `page[size]` that isn't a positive integer, a cursor
+that isn't one of the list's (e.g. one of another list), both `page[after]`
+and `page[before]`, `per_page` with `page[...]`, or another `page[...]`
+parameter. Blank `page[...]` values are ignored.
 
